nodecode-nats is a rewrite of atbrace's atbrace/hermes-jetstream:
https://github.com/atbrace/hermes-jetstream
commit 4508957d1889ddb625c8bfb1eef7c822f2761349
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research / atbrace contributors (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's two session-facing verbs and every gate
around them — nats_publish (a literal, allowlisted subject; a payload
scanned for credential shapes; a thin envelope with a schema, a kind, a
timestamp and a per-process nonce; a size cap) and nats_fetch (one bounded
pull, acknowledged as it reads) — plus the config surface they run on
(servers, stream, both allowlists, timeout, payload cap) and a /nats
command that reports it and probes the server.

What was left out: the platform adapter and its durable wake routes, the
runtime route store the agent could add to, the in-session watch, and the
lifecycle tap that mirrored Hermes events onto hermes.> subjects. Those
need a Hermes gateway's long-lived host — a process that stays resident to
receive turns — and this cell is a verb in a session rather than a process
of its own, so a wake here is a durable consumer the operator runs against
the same stream. The delivery rails that went with the adapter (rate
buckets, coalescing, the wake-claims ledger, reconnect supervision) are
left out for that reason.

The NATS wire protocol is spoken directly over the image's socket support:
the rewrite carries no client library and no CLI.
